  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-13e92c95ad163c91": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-817",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "There are several varieties of lanterns in use in Palestine at the present day. One of these commonly used is made of waxed linen, or even of paper, stretched over rings of wire, and having a top and bottom of tinned copper. When folded the candle projects above the top of the fo…"
      },
      "c-17f0756ea2d01e80": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-816",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Gardens were made outside of the cities. The rabbins assign as a reason for this the foul smells which arose from the weeds and from the manure which was necessary for fertilizing. “Upon this account there were no gardens in the city, (some few gardens of roses excepted, which ha…"
      },
      "c-3c96afcbdf9c0541": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-819",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Charcoal is of ancient origin, and is still used for fuel in Palestine, though not so commonly employed for that purpose as sticks and other articles. See notes on 1 Kings 17:10 (#307); Psalm 58:9 (#646) and Matthew 6:30. It was doubtless the material used for the fire spoken of…"
      },
      "c-7dbd14046486b17c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-818",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Women were often employed by the ancients as porters. Classical writers make frequent allusion to the custom. It is mentioned also in the account of Peter’s deliverance from prison, wherein it is stated that the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, had a female porter. See Act…"
      }
    },
